WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:04.980
Some of the most popular TV shows of all time
almost never had the chance to find a fan

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:07.300
base, as the networks nearly cancelled them.

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:12.019
And other hits actually were cancelled before
gaining an unexpected second life.

00:00:12.020 --> 00:00:16.180
Here's a look at some of the most popular
TV shows that were so close to being cancelled

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:16.680
forever.

00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:19.560
It turns out Friends was almost not "there
for you."

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:23.780
Co-creator David Crane recently explained
to The Huffington Post that the show, which

00:00:23.790 --> 00:00:28.550
went on to run for 10 seasons and ranks as
one of the most-watched programs in U.S. history,

00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:29.660
didn't test well at first.

00:00:30.009 --> 00:00:33.740
Apparently, it was considered to be geared
towards too young of an audience, had too

00:00:33.740 --> 00:00:38.460
many sexual innuendos, and featured characters
that were only moderately likable to test

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:38.960
audiences.

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:43.460
NBC viewed it as a lackluster effort, but
didn't have much else to fall back on for

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:47.520
its fall schedule, and Crane and co-creator
Marta Kauffman stood by their concept.

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:48.760
Crane said,

00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:50.410
"The show was always the six friends.

00:00:50.410 --> 00:00:52.180
And we were prepared to live or die by that."

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:54.380
"That is how God intended it."

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:59.560
Despite its critical acclaim and a small but
dedicated fan following, NBC's Community was

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:03.411
dangerously close to being canceled in the
middle of its third season, as the network

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:07.420
put the show on hiatus with no return date
mentioned in its promotional materials.

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:12.040
Fans of the series eventually succeeded in
pressing for a renewal of the show, it ran

00:01:12.049 --> 00:01:15.160
for two more seasons at NBC before being picked
up by Yahoo!

00:01:15.160 --> 00:01:20.270
Screen to avoid yet another cancellation scare,
finishing out its sixth season on the digital

00:01:20.270 --> 00:01:20.860
platform.

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:26.100
The show did not return for a seventh season,
but it has long boasted itself as a "six seasons

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:30.490
and a movie" experience, so a feature film
follow-up is still spinning in the rumor mill.

00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:36.159
"We wait a little bit, let them explore their
awesome careers, then get together for an

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:37.160
incredible movie."

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:43.660
In another instance of NBC almost underestimating
its TV gold reserves with a premature cancellation,

00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:48.280
the network reportedly only signed on for
a four-episode order of what was then titled

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:51.020
The Seinfeld Chronicles, according to Business
Insider.

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:55.360
Rick Ludwin, who headed up late-night programming
for the network, ultimately convinced execs

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:59.790
to produce four more episodes and rerun the
pilot, which then received significantly higher

00:01:59.790 --> 00:02:02.280
ratings and justified ordering a second season.

00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:08.120
Seinfeld ultimately ran for nine seasons and
was the most-watched TV show from 1994 to

00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:10.860
1995 and 1997 to 1998.

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:12.420
"You're very funny Jerry.

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:14.100
That's what I always tell people.

00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:16.100
Jerry Seinfeld's a funny guy!"

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:21.240
The story of the Dillon, Texas' football teams
with "clear eyes and full hearts" almost did

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:25.680
lose after its second season ratings were
low enough to justify its early cancellation

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:26.500
on NBC.

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:30.860
But just like the Panthers, the network showed
some serious heart by negotiating a deal with

00:02:30.860 --> 00:02:36.210
DirecTV to co-produce its third, fourth, and
fifth seasons on DirecTV's 101 Network instead

00:02:36.210 --> 00:02:37.320
of cutting it altogether.

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:39.040
"You gotta get the fans involved.

00:02:39.260 --> 00:02:43.180
You know like sometimes people mail stuff
to networks to show how passionate they are.

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:46.340
They sent lightbulbs to save Friday Night
Lights."

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:50.770
The show is now a cult favorite and was a
notable critical success, despite its less-than-ideal

00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:51.440
audience numbers.

00:02:51.940 --> 00:02:53.380
"So I got that going for me.

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:54.600
And wine."

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:58.940
In another case of an original series' fan
base pulling out all of the creative stops

00:02:58.950 --> 00:03:03.380
to get a network to greenlight another season,
followers of HBO's critically-acclaimed but

00:03:03.380 --> 00:03:07.380
ratings-plagued drama series The Leftovers
showed up at the network's headquarters in

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:11.600
New York City dressed as the show's Guilty
Remnant faction, a group of people who wear

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:15.940
all white, chain smoke, and wordlessly picket
events as a form of civil disobedience.

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:21.060
According to Variety, HBO, citing their respect
for such fan enthusiasm and commitment to

00:03:21.070 --> 00:03:25.080
creator Damon Lindelof's vision for closing
out the series, renewed it for a third and

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:28.020
final season which brought the story to its
preordained conclusion.

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:30.840
"So this is the last time we're ever gonna
see each other, huh?"

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:31.580
"What do you mean?"

00:03:31.820 --> 00:03:32.820
"Cause the world is ending."

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:34.040
"Oh, yeah."

00:03:34.540 --> 00:03:39.000
In another instance of a series being rescued
from its network's dust bin, Fox's Arrested

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:43.220
Development was shelved after three seasons
thanks to a ratings dip in the second season

00:03:43.220 --> 00:03:46.800
and a continued decline in season three before
it was ultimately canceled.

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:50.840
But fans of the quirky comedy series made
their hopes for more perfectly clear, leading

00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:55.020
to Netflix purchasing the rights and producing
a highly touted fourth season for its own

00:03:55.020 --> 00:03:57.200
originals lineup in 2013.

00:03:57.420 --> 00:03:59.520
A fifth season followed in 2018.

00:03:59.720 --> 00:04:01.060
"Wait til you see it, Michael!"

